WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:05.190
- Today on "Drew",
secrets will be revealed.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.697
It's the most memorable
moments from season one.

00:00:09.697 --> 00:00:11.741
- You have changed my life.

00:00:11.741 --> 00:00:13.000
(audience cheers)

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.390
- I had so much that I wanted
to talk to Oprah about.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.600
I actually didn't sleep the night before.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.220
It all starts now.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.387
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.710
- [Presenter] Please
welcome Drew Barrymore.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:28.100
- Hello.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.470
Hi, everyone.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:32.880
The countdown is on for season two

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.160
of "The Drew Barrymore Show."

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.130
It all kicks off at exactly one week,

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.220
Monday, September 13th, and
you're not gonna wanna miss it.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.470
We've got huge guests,

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:48.470
unbelievable surprises,
and a live studio audience.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:53.600
I can't wait to see your
beautiful faces here in person.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.710
Who knows, you might be on camera.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.890
Until then, let's take a look back

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:00.220
at our favorite moments from season one,

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.700
with some inside scoop from yours truly,

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:08.010
a little Barrymore behind
the scenes if you will.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:12.230
First up, one of the most
impactful moments for me

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:15.650
was interviewing my interview icon.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.600
She is daytime royalty.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:19.440
Truly.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.466
No one comes close, and I can only hope

00:01:22.466 --> 00:01:25.750
to honor her by doing my best.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:30.750
Here's my chat with the
indomitable Oprah Winfrey.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:32.670
Welcome to the show.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.143
Dare I say, maybe you've seen it.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:37.070
- Yes, I have.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:40.130
And may I say, dare I say,

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.530
seeing it actually changed my life.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.300
You have changed my life.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.740
You certainly changed my work life

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:50.620
because I was there for
that very first show

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:53.700
where you beamed in Cameron,

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.350
and there you all were.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:02:00.350
And we didn't know that you
all weren't in the same space.

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:03.267
I watched that and I said,

00:02:03.267 --> 00:02:06.410
"Hey, why can't we do what Drew did?"

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:07.637
And since then,

00:02:07.637 --> 00:02:09.470
"Why can't we do what Drew did?"

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:13.560
And since that time, for
"Oprah Conversations"

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.630
for Apple TV, the first time I did it

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:20.270
was with President Barack Obama

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:22.727
talking about his book "Promised Land."

00:02:23.756 --> 00:02:27.090
And there we were sitting
right across from each other

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:30.050
in my living room in
front of the fireplace.

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:33.500
And I said then, "This
is because of you Drew."

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.422
So, thank you, pioneer woman.

00:02:35.422 --> 00:02:37.737
(audience applauds)
(audience cheers)

00:02:37.737 --> 00:02:41.450
- I have to thank you so
much because you didn't need

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.550
to give us the credit, but you did.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:44.930
And for those who are not aware,

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:48.850
here's a little taped
piece on the technology

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:52.115
of which Oprah and I are talking about.

00:02:52.115 --> 00:02:52.948
(upbeat music)

00:02:52.948 --> 00:02:55.910
When we launched this show
in the middle of a pandemic,

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:57.550
we had to get creative.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:59.090
With the magic of technology,

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:01.800
we came up with a way
to transport our guests

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.020
from our LA studio right here to New York.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:06.290
How cool is that?

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.760
We knew one person who thought so too.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:09.863
It's Oprah!

00:03:10.824 --> 00:03:11.920
It's Oprah!

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:13.284
It's Oprah!

00:03:13.284 --> 00:03:14.534
Oprah approved!

00:03:15.773 --> 00:03:16.820
Oprah like-y.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:19.640
Yes, the Oprah was inspired to use

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.540
a similar green screen technology

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.180
to interview guests like
Chip and Joanna Gaines,

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.410
and of course, President Barack Obama.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:28.770
- Can she see me yet?

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:29.830
- I can.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:32.320
- This is pretty cool
technology you got here.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.260
- [Drew] Oh, why, thank
you, Mr. President.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.620
And thank you, Oprah.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.790
Listen, we may not be able
to be in the same room,

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:42.192
but we can sure feel
like it and look like it.

00:03:42.192 --> 00:03:43.556
(audience applauds)
(audience cheers)

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:46.330
I thank you so much,

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:49.360
because I'm gonna be really honest,

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.220
I haven't really admitted this out loud,

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:56.577
but it was really scary
for me to be a girl,

00:03:57.970 --> 00:03:59.870
a woman, I don't know what I am,

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:04.360
a female asking for
people to take this risk

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:08.370
and spend this money and ask
ourselves these questions.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:11.450
And I'm so grateful to my bosses

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.130
that they allowed us to do this,

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:16.150
because this was a check

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:18.810
and a risk away from not happening.

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:23.110
And I wanna ask you, is
this where we're going?

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:24.210
The water's out of the bottle.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.780
People are not gonna travel
the way they used to.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:30.130
People have gotten comfortable
with interview styles,

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:31.910
some are gonna show up, some aren't.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:33.500
You're gonna have access to more people

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:35.210
than you ever did in some ways,

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:39.050
'cause the technology, it's
all gonna be maybe a mixed bag.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:42.130
And if it is, don't we
have to make it the best

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:46.330
and most compelling television
in honor of the audience?

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:48.520
Yeah, I, 1000% agree with that.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:50.174
And I think that's why I say

00:04:50.174 --> 00:04:52.130
you literally have changed my life.

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:53.127
And when people say that, I say,

00:04:53.127 --> 00:04:54.710
"'Cause, listen, after Drew,

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:56.534
uh-uh, I'm not going nowhere."

00:04:56.534 --> 00:04:57.890
(audience laughs)

00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:01.410
And so, the truth is you're
able to have better access.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.620
And I think there will certainly be times

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:06.750
in both of our lives,

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.980
and in our careers,

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.760
when being directly in front of the person

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:17.350
makes a world of difference
and may be necessary.

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:20.590
But I think that in terms of having access

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:24.960
to anybody in the world at any time,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:27.483
what you all did in that first show,

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:30.960
lit the path for changing it forever.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:33.161
And I'm sure we would have done it today,

00:05:33.161 --> 00:05:34.620
but where there's no studio.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:36.540
So I'm like in the middle
of nowhere out here,

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:38.270
you need a studio to be able to do it.

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:39.583
- You really do.

00:05:39.583 --> 00:05:41.130
- If there was a studio within 500 miles,

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:42.070
I would have done it.

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:43.240
- I'm so glad.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:46.000
And may we do it again in the future?

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.350
Because I was so glad
to see you embracing us,

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:53.060
you acknowledging us and us
moving forward in this way,

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:55.510
because it does become a new normal.

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:59.288
And for the girl who felt really,

00:05:59.288 --> 00:06:01.440
honestly, again, this is the part

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:04.160
I don't talk about, is
when you're fighting

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:07.320
for things in business,
it's not comfortable,

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.660
especially when they're not done.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:11.400
You feel like the messy one,

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:13.090
you feel like the one who might be

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:15.980
spending people's money
in an improper way.

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:18.150
You don't know if the
risk is gonna pay off,

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.050
but you get into your convictions
and you fight for them.

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:23.570
And so, when I saw you
embracing the technology,

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:26.320
it told that person who
hung up the phone that day,

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:27.467
'cause I remember saying,

00:06:27.467 --> 00:06:29.940
"Listen, you guys, we have got to do this.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:33.450
We have got to find a new way
for our future of television.

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:34.850
This is not funny.

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:36.870
We are in a really unprecedented time

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.850
and we can't go on with
the home show forever.

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:41.040
We just can't."

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.226
- You did the thing that I had to do

00:06:43.226 --> 00:06:45.390
a thousand times over the years.

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:47.642
And when I first started,

00:06:47.642 --> 00:06:52.540
so let me applaud you for doing
the thing that every person,

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:57.400
particularly every woman needs
to learn as soon as possible.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:02.400
Your gut is right for you.

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:06.410
So when you feel something in your gut,

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:11.040
and you think, "Wow, I need
to really move on that."

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:14.120
That means it is right for you.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:17.030
It doesn't mean that it's
right for everybody else.

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:20.010
And I think it takes
women in particularly,

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:24.680
a long, long time to be
affirmed in their own beliefs.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:26.810
And that is the reason actually

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:31.460
we were the number one
talk show for 25 years,

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:36.460
is because it always came
back to what do I feel?

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:41.170
What do I feel is my real
intention in doing this?

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:44.270
And I cannot do it if it's not true to me,

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:45.370
even if everybody else says.

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:48.090
And many times, Drew, producers
would come in with ideas,

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.490
and I would say, "I can't do that.

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:52.914
I don't know how I can sit in that chair,

00:07:52.914 --> 00:07:55.030
and keep a straight face,

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:57.511
and do that, and do that.

00:07:57.511 --> 00:07:59.090
I'm not gonna be able to do that."

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:03.470
So, I applaud you for going with your gut

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:06.830
'cause your gut is always right for you.

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:11.510
- Well, I think you've given
so much courage to everyone,

00:08:11.510 --> 00:08:16.330
especially women to fight
for what we believe in.

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:20.296
And I was gonna ask you
if you had any advice,

00:08:20.296 --> 00:08:23.720
may I ask when you ended your first season

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:27.530
and you knew you were rounding
the corner into season two,

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:30.270
what were you telling yourself?

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:32.563
- I actually was telling myself,

00:08:33.494 --> 00:08:36.340
"Self, you was right,"

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:40.130
because most people don't know this now,

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:44.160
but when we launched in 1986,

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:50.910
I had been a local talk show
in Chicago for two years.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:55.550
What I understood was
that there's a commonality

00:08:57.098 --> 00:08:58.710
in our human experience

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:01.160
and that people are no
different in Chicago

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:02.590
than they are in New York,

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:03.980
than they are in Idaho,

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:05.780
than they are in Rhode Island

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:07.550
when it comes to the core.

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:11.300
Now everybody's so divided
about their politics,

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:12.810
and their beliefs, and so forth.

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:15.050
But I still do believe at the core.

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:16.460
We're all the same.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:19.870
If you ask blue states,
red states, purple states,

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:21.580
what really matters to you?

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:23.360
The answer is the same.

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:24.900
People care about their families,

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:27.450
they care about being
able to live decent lives,

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:30.190
they care about being
able to do that in comfort

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:32.513
and do that with a sense of peace.

00:09:34.157 --> 00:09:38.533
And I still think in spite
of all the craziness,

00:09:38.533 --> 00:09:39.700
in spite of all the divisions,

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:42.680
which is what everybody
talks about all the time,

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:45.970
is that we are more alike
than we are different,

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:48.440
which is a very famous Maya Angelou line.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:49.970
She used to say that all the time.

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:51.920
And in our humanness,

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.770
we are, we all are just
seeking the same things.

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:56.327
And that's what this platform,

00:09:56.327 --> 00:09:59.670
"The Drew Barrymore
Show" offers every day,

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:03.430
is a chance for those of
us of like-mindedness,

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:05.740
those of us who are seeking the joy,

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:07.959
who wants to wear some bows.

00:10:07.959 --> 00:10:09.690
(all laugh)

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:11.680
- I went extra jaunty for you.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:14.422
I was like, bows and flowers, it's Oprah.

00:10:14.422 --> 00:10:18.354
I'm surprised I don't
have bells on and a boa.

00:10:18.354 --> 00:10:19.430
(audience laughs)
(audience applauds)

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:22.980
I had so much that I wanted
to talk to Oprah about.

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:25.490
I actually didn't sleep the night before.

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:28.786
Stay tuned for part two of our chat.

00:10:28.786 --> 00:10:31.953
(bright upbeat music)

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:40.960
- What I realized is that
Gayle was my regulation,

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:43.022
makes me wanna cry now thinking about it,

00:10:43.022 --> 00:10:45.570
and is still the person in my life.

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:48.120
I have probably three people in my life

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:50.430
who are gonna tell me the
truth, no matter what.

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:51.490
Here's the bottom line,

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:55.593
that really was the
hugest epiphany for me.

00:11:01.341 --> 00:11:04.550
(bright upbeat music)

00:11:04.550 --> 00:11:05.383
- Welcome back.

00:11:05.383 --> 00:11:07.910
Right now, we're diving
into our favorite moments

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:10.460
from season one of "The
Drew Barrymore Show"

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:14.740
with some exclusive host
commentary from me, your host,

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:18.520
and no guest compares
to the queen of daytime,

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:20.060
Miss Oprah Winfrey.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:21.580
And of course, I had to talk to her

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:24.230
about our mutual BFF, Gayle King,

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:26.040
and all of her favorite things.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.710
But Oprah had a new book out

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:31.170
about how our brains process trauma.

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:34.830
And while I love to talk
about the tough stuff,

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:37.400
and who better to talk about that

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:39.000
than with Oprah Winfrey?

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.800
Please take a look at part
two of our conversation.

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:44.310
You now have "Oprah Daily,"

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:46.430
which I happened to mention to Gayle,

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:48.510
that I thought that the "Oprah Daily,"

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:51.700
the daily word, it's so brilliant.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:53.890
- Well, thank you, thank you.

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:55.960
I actually came up with that

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:57.480
because I wanted it to be...

00:11:58.967 --> 00:12:00.930
what I realized is that
"The Oprah Show" was a habit

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:02.670
just like "The Drew Barrymore Show."

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:05.260
People were creating a habit around that.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:06.340
And that's what you want.

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:07.660
That's your core viewer,

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:09.823
that's who you're speaking to every day.

00:12:11.303 --> 00:12:15.430
And the idea of having a
daily inspiration point,

00:12:15.430 --> 00:12:18.547
a daily quote, a daily something

00:12:18.547 --> 00:12:23.040
that you can rely on to
give you just a little lift.

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:24.770
My whole goal throughout my life,

00:12:24.770 --> 00:12:26.730
even before I could articulate it,

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:29.760
is how do you bring a
light into people's lives?

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:32.320
How do you let them see
the best of themselves?

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:35.200
How do you allow them to grow

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:37.510
into becoming more of who they are,

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:40.830
which is what every one of us
is actually aspiring to do,

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:44.530
whether you can articulate
that for yourself or not.

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:47.870
You just wanna become more and more solid.

00:12:47.870 --> 00:12:51.530
You wanna have the ability to
have the fullest expression

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:54.423
of yourself as a human being.

00:12:55.943 --> 00:12:59.935
And you do that by habits, by rituals,

00:12:59.935 --> 00:13:03.790
by creating a space where
you can give back to yourself

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:05.810
in a way that allows you to flourish.

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:10.810
So, we are growing it into what
I know will be in the future

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:14.910
is a daily spot of
inspiration for everybody.

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:16.510
So, thank you for mentioning that.

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:17.860
- We need you on the daily.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:20.160
I listened to your Sirius XM channel

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:21.823
through both my pregnancies,

00:13:23.263 --> 00:13:26.630
and it was my daily north star,

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:28.251
it was my daily fix.

00:13:28.251 --> 00:13:29.300
- Well, thank you for that.

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:30.360
- It's true.

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:31.970
I'll never forget the story you told

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:34.680
about Gayle getting the
water for the traffic cop

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:38.795
and how that moment was
her being such an important

00:13:38.795 --> 00:13:40.790
and good parent to her son,

00:13:40.790 --> 00:13:43.427
because she was showing him
the kind of person to be.

00:13:43.427 --> 00:13:46.760
And that gave me hope,
because I felt so lost.

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:48.940
Like, what blueprint do I have to pull on

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:50.330
to become a parent?

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:52.860
I was scared out of my mind.

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:55.350
And I thought hearing that story,

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:57.480
if I can be an example to my kids,

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:00.531
maybe I don't have to draw
from my past or even fight it,

00:14:00.531 --> 00:14:03.190
I can try to be myself.

00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:08.190
And that can give me
hope to be a good parent.

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:10.873
That story literally changed my life.

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:13.120
So thank you for that.

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:15.787
- Well Gayle is to date,
and she always says,

00:14:15.787 --> 00:14:17.717
"Well, all parents do, and all parents."

00:14:17.717 --> 00:14:18.670
I said, "No, they don't."

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:23.670
Gayle is to date the best
model parent I have ever seen.

00:14:23.690 --> 00:14:27.900
I've never seen anybody who was,

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:32.773
more engaged, more
respectful of her children.

00:14:32.773 --> 00:14:34.980
And when she first had kids,

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:36.450
because we were best friends

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:39.810
and met at a television
station in Baltimore,

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:42.030
and once she married
and had kids, I thought,

00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:44.210
well, there's gonna be
distance in our relationship

00:14:44.210 --> 00:14:45.967
'cause she's not gonna have time

00:14:45.967 --> 00:14:48.843
to devote to our friendship,

00:14:50.047 --> 00:14:52.080
and that did not happen.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:55.150
But what did happen every time
she was on the phone with me

00:14:55.150 --> 00:14:57.920
and one of her children entered
the room, she would stop,

00:14:57.920 --> 00:14:59.550
she would put the phone down.

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:01.060
Even if she couldn't put the phone down,

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:02.910
they would be acknowledged.

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:04.500
She was present there for them.

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:07.250
She would say, "Mommy's on
the phone now with Auntie O"

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:09.950
And, "Morning, Bear."

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:11.413
She calls Kirby Bear.

00:15:11.413 --> 00:15:13.527
There was an acknowledgement,

00:15:13.527 --> 00:15:16.350
and what I would have
to say over the years,

00:15:16.350 --> 00:15:17.710
a mutual respect.

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:19.820
And I know we're gonna be talking later

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:22.490
about the subject of what happened to you.

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:25.541
But one of the things that
Dr. Perry says in this book

00:15:25.541 --> 00:15:29.480
is that when children
grow up and they realize

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:34.480
that they are not getting
the respect that they deserve

00:15:34.510 --> 00:15:38.470
from their parents, it
changes who they are.

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:39.430
What your kids want,

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:42.690
and this is actually true of everybody,

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:44.890
they wanna know that you're here,

00:15:44.890 --> 00:15:45.950
that you see me,

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:48.990
that you see me and you hear
me and that I matter to you.

00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:52.200
And that is true for everybody
else in your life, too.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:54.530
- Speaking of this extraordinary book

00:15:54.530 --> 00:15:56.040
that I've been reading,

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:59.150
and I will forever have the chart

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:03.410
that Dr. Perry put in the book as far as-

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:04.330
- The upside down triangle?

00:16:04.330 --> 00:16:05.700
- The upside down triangle.

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:10.700
What you guys talk about
as far as the impression

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:14.560
that children are left
with at such an earlier age

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:16.890
than I think was spoken about

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:19.700
in at least my growing up,

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:23.740
I think, even into almost having kids,

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:25.953
I feel like everybody kind of went,

00:16:25.953 --> 00:16:28.681
they won't notice when they're young.

00:16:28.681 --> 00:16:33.090
And I did not understand
why that was the notion.

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:35.310
It didn't seem right,
it didn't feel right,

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:37.240
but I wanted to believe it

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:39.570
because that's what so
many people had said.

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:40.610
- Here's the bottom line,

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:45.110
that really was the
hugest epiphany for me.

00:16:45.110 --> 00:16:47.850
All these years, I've done multiple shows

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:51.987
where people say exactly
what you just said, Drew,

00:16:51.987 --> 00:16:53.430
"Oh, they won't remember."

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:56.093
I know, yes, I'm in an
abusive relationship,

00:16:58.646 --> 00:16:59.750
and the kids are young,

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:01.410
when they get older, I'm gonna move.

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:02.920
I'm gonna get out of the relationship.

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:06.830
The most damage is done
from zero to two months,

00:17:06.830 --> 00:17:07.960
'cause that's the time

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:10.260
when the brain is taking in everything.

00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:11.920
And anybody who's ever had children,

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:14.006
you all know this because you get to watch

00:17:14.006 --> 00:17:17.960
that whole language socialization process,

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:20.940
how do they learn the word
dada, how do they learn mamma,

00:17:20.940 --> 00:17:23.240
how do they learn spoon,
how do they learn?

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:25.310
So, as they're taking in all of that

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:28.800
when the brain is the
most open and receptive,

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:31.930
they're also taking in
all the other stuff.

00:17:31.930 --> 00:17:34.180
That changed everything for me,

00:17:34.180 --> 00:17:36.970
in terms of how people
are raising their kids

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:41.140
and how we see that what you do

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:44.093
in front of the youngest children matters.

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:49.610
- I have a question, which
is that when I was born,

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:52.230
my dad, he had split with my mom.

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:54.900
And I just didn't know him.

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:56.250
He was very abusive.

00:17:56.250 --> 00:17:58.743
He would come around, he
would trash the house.

00:17:59.780 --> 00:18:00.883
He was scary.

00:18:02.529 --> 00:18:05.920
And there are a lot of people,

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:08.360
and you share a story

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:10.290
of what happened the first night

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:13.320
that you stayed with
your mom in Milwaukee.

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:18.320
And for people who have had a parent,

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:22.130
one or two that did not want to be

00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:24.770
in their child's lives for
whatever their reasons were,

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:26.410
which is valid to them,

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:29.100
very hard for a child to understand,

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:32.240
what do you talk through with yourself

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:34.980
or other people who have had that kind of

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:39.880
start to their lives, who
deal with that kind of trauma?

00:18:39.880 --> 00:18:42.250
You talk about the eyes
and the being wanted

00:18:42.250 --> 00:18:44.350
and the being fed up, right?

00:18:44.350 --> 00:18:46.780
What happens when you're not?

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:50.940
- So here's the good
news about all of this,

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:52.590
because all of us have had things

00:18:53.720 --> 00:18:56.430
to happen to us in our lives

00:18:56.430 --> 00:19:00.403
that caused us to feel unwanted,

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:05.180
and not important.

00:19:05.180 --> 00:19:07.280
Everybody has at some point in your life,

00:19:07.280 --> 00:19:10.600
even if you come from the
best of circumstances,

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:15.090
it is possible to take
your post-traumatic pain

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:18.580
and turn that into post-traumatic power.

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:20.700
And post-traumatic wisdom.

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:22.920
I know this for sure,

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:25.480
that whatever has happened to you,

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:30.480
can also be for you if you allow it to be.

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:33.780
The thing that has healed me the most

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:36.990
is taking the pain that I endured,

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:41.730
suffered, survived as a child
and turning that inside out.

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:44.890
So that is why I created
my school specifically

00:19:44.890 --> 00:19:47.270
looking for girls who were just like me,

00:19:47.270 --> 00:19:49.248
who came from poor backgrounds,

00:19:49.248 --> 00:19:52.003
who came from dysfunctional backgrounds,

00:19:53.192 --> 00:19:55.280
who many times felt unwanted, unloved.

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:57.510
How do I create an environment for them?

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:59.360
How do I give back?

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:00.860
This is how you save yourself.

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:05.420
How do I give back that
which I was not given.

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:07.060
And when you can do that,

00:20:07.060 --> 00:20:09.580
you begin to fill the
hole inside yourself.

00:20:09.580 --> 00:20:14.580
That giving back has become
really what my legacy is.

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:16.540
And I will share this with you,

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:18.476
that I was sitting with Maya Angelou

00:20:18.476 --> 00:20:21.430
after I'd come back from
the opening of my school.

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:23.450
And I said, oh, this
is gonna be the school.

00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:25.510
This is gonna be my greatest legacy.

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:26.613
I mean, these girls,

00:20:27.549 --> 00:20:29.470
this is what my life was for.

00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:32.900
And she said, "You have no
idea what your legacy will be."

00:20:32.900 --> 00:20:34.440
And I said, "Oh, no, no, no, no, Maya,

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:35.820
you should have been there.

00:20:35.820 --> 00:20:37.850
I'm telling you it's gonna be the school."

00:20:37.850 --> 00:20:42.390
And she said, "I said you have no idea

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:45.490
what your legacy will be.

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:48.650
Your legacy is every life you touch.

00:20:48.650 --> 00:20:50.750
Your legacy isn't your name on a school

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:52.240
or any other building.

00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:53.680
Your legacy is every life you touch."

00:20:53.680 --> 00:20:55.520
So I say this to you, Drew Barrymore,

00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:57.327
your legacy, this is what Maya said to me,

00:20:57.327 --> 00:20:59.941
"Everybody who's ever
watched a show and decided

00:20:59.941 --> 00:21:01.760
I'm gonna go back to school.

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:03.040
I'm gonna go on a diet.

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:04.460
I'm gonna treat myself better.

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:06.750
I'm gonna leave an abusive relationship.

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:10.080
Every life that you have touched

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:12.110
that has experienced your voice

00:21:12.110 --> 00:21:14.210
and the power of your platform.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:16.517
That is your legacy."

00:21:16.517 --> 00:21:19.090
(audience applauds)

