- There you go, a little
bit of vegetable stock.

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:30.580
- Here we go.

00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:32.010
Yeah, baby.

00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:32.870
Lovely.

00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:34.010
So in the meanwhile,

00:25:34.010 --> 00:25:35.780
we had some of our quinoa going,

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:36.700
what I like to do is

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:39.530
I start toasting it
first in a dry skillet.

00:25:39.530 --> 00:25:42.230
It adds this really nice
nuttiness to the dish.

00:25:42.230 --> 00:25:45.300
- I have to say, I've
not seen people do that.

00:25:45.300 --> 00:25:46.560
- Yeah, it's really lovely.

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:49.030
45 seconds in a hot, dry pan.

00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:50.780
- Dry, that's the key.

00:25:50.780 --> 00:25:52.300
Don't put oil in it.

00:25:52.300 --> 00:25:53.810
- No, no oil, no nothing.

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:56.450
And then you're gonna add
some liquid into that.

00:25:56.450 --> 00:25:58.840
We use vegetable stock
and you're gonna bring

00:25:58.840 --> 00:26:00.830
that to a boil and then
turn it down to simmer.

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:01.970
And you're gonna mix it

00:26:01.970 --> 00:26:05.330
and stir it and cook it
for about 18 minutes.

00:26:05.330 --> 00:26:07.970
So we're gonna take our puree.

00:26:07.970 --> 00:26:08.980
- You're gonna take your puree,

00:26:08.980 --> 00:26:11.230
which I recommend folding in.

00:26:11.230 --> 00:26:12.530
- It feels like a hug and a bowl

00:26:12.530 --> 00:26:13.907
is what I like to say, which is-

00:26:13.907 --> 00:26:17.320
- I want a hug in a bowl, Pilar.

00:26:17.320 --> 00:26:19.300
Am I gonna start arranging?

00:26:19.300 --> 00:26:21.760
- Yes, please arrange a range away.

00:26:21.760 --> 00:26:23.970
- Now, these are trumpet mushrooms.

00:26:23.970 --> 00:26:25.300
- They are trumpet mushrooms.

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:27.120
I love mushrooms.

00:26:27.120 --> 00:26:31.470
- I'm excited to bring what
should be a really heavy,

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:35.890
heavy dish to this
lightness that is so healthy

00:26:35.890 --> 00:26:36.810
and good for your body,

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:39.140
but still gives all of that satisfaction.

00:26:39.140 --> 00:26:40.390
And thank you.
- And it's delicious.

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:42.198
- For the satisfaction, baby.

00:26:42.198 --> 00:26:46.508
(audience cheers)
(upbeat music)

00:26:46.508 --> 00:26:47.407
- I love you.

00:26:47.407 --> 00:26:48.240
- I love you.

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:49.400
They taste like peanut butter.

00:26:49.400 --> 00:26:51.303
- And with a salt on top, I mean.

00:26:52.694 --> 00:26:53.783
- Mm-hm.

00:26:53.783 --> 00:26:55.350
- Oh my God.

00:26:55.350 --> 00:26:56.990
This is a true original,

00:26:56.990 --> 00:26:59.210
just like you, Pilar.
- Thank you.

00:26:59.210 --> 00:27:00.043
- I love you.

00:27:00.043 --> 00:27:01.060
I couldn't do it alone.

00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:02.573
I could only do it with Pilar.

00:27:03.723 --> 00:27:06.533
(audience applauds)

00:27:06.533 --> 00:27:08.980
I love you, Pilar, so much.

00:27:08.980 --> 00:27:10.348
We'll be right back.

00:27:10.348 --> 00:27:12.931
(upbeat music)

00:27:19.249 --> 00:27:21.832
(upbeat music)

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:27.690
Even the stars who come on
our show, get star struck.

00:27:27.690 --> 00:27:29.770
Here's someone we love talking about

00:27:29.770 --> 00:27:32.270
a time they met someone they love.

00:27:32.270 --> 00:27:33.570
- The biggest time I was starstruck.

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:35.550
I was invited to an after party

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:37.940
at the Russian Tea Room
to meet Paul McCartney,

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:39.080
talking to Paul McCartney

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:41.297
is just bizarre because he'll be like,

00:27:41.297 --> 00:27:43.130
"John used to sit like that."

00:27:43.130 --> 00:27:45.200
And you go, "John Lennon.

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:46.033
That's who you're talking about?"

00:27:46.033 --> 00:27:47.480
Yeah, 'cause you're Paul McCartney.

00:27:47.480 --> 00:27:49.120
And so I just kept like
kind of stuttering,

00:27:49.120 --> 00:27:50.700
just staring and kind of blinking a lot.

00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:52.460
Ask him all the dumb questions you ask

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:53.976
about the Beatles and Paul McCartney.

00:27:53.976 --> 00:27:56.440
I remember I told my mom,
I might be going to this.

00:27:56.440 --> 00:27:59.660
My mom's like, "If you go
to the Russian Tea Room,

00:27:59.660 --> 00:28:02.550
you have to get a Moscow mule.

00:28:02.550 --> 00:28:03.763
The owner comes over and says,

00:28:03.763 --> 00:28:04.670
"Can I get you anything?"

00:28:04.670 --> 00:28:07.957
And I go, "Can I be a Moscow mule?"

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:11.030
And Paul would be like, "He's cool.

00:28:11.030 --> 00:28:13.800
Do you want to reform the
Beatles with you in it?"

00:28:13.800 --> 00:28:14.690
He doesn't even hear me.

00:28:14.690 --> 00:28:16.820
And the rest is history

00:28:16.820 --> 00:28:19.600
and we currently don't speak.

00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:20.590
- You know what?

00:28:20.590 --> 00:28:24.203
We all freak out over
someone at some point.

00:28:30.159 --> 00:28:32.742
(upbeat music)

00:28:33.730 --> 00:28:34.820
Welcome back.

00:28:34.820 --> 00:28:37.550
When we launched "The
Drew Barrymore Show",

00:28:37.550 --> 00:28:40.030
we were in the thick of the pandemic.

00:28:40.030 --> 00:28:43.410
And so much was uncertain at the time,

00:28:43.410 --> 00:28:47.730
everything including school
teachers, principals,

00:28:47.730 --> 00:28:50.310
and parents were stretched so thin.

00:28:50.310 --> 00:28:52.640
They were dealing with the impossible.

00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:55.090
And on the show, we
talked to many educators

00:28:55.090 --> 00:28:58.810
about how they were dealing
with those unprecedented times.

00:28:58.810 --> 00:29:01.550
And there was one teacher
whose story really stuck

00:29:01.550 --> 00:29:03.280
with me and we were lucky enough

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:06.010
to help him continue to help

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:09.278
all his students at Aiton Elementary.

00:29:09.278 --> 00:29:11.460
Take a look at this.

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:13.590
Your story is so incredible.

00:29:13.590 --> 00:29:16.000
Like tell us about your
school first of all.

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:17.239
- Yeah.

00:29:17.239 --> 00:29:19.930
So Aiton Elementary is a
school in Washington, DC.

00:29:19.930 --> 00:29:22.491
It is in ward seven, Northeast DC.

00:29:22.491 --> 00:29:27.200
This community has been
underserved for several years.

00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:31.010
However, this community is
filled with such cultural wealth.

00:29:31.010 --> 00:29:33.370
So I like to describe it as a
culturally wealthy community,

00:29:33.370 --> 00:29:35.280
although it is a Title One school

00:29:35.280 --> 00:29:36.820
and majority of the students

00:29:36.820 --> 00:29:38.410
are receiving free and reduced lunch,

00:29:38.410 --> 00:29:41.963
but it is such a loving
and caring community,

00:29:42.920 --> 00:29:47.150
down from the parents, to the
teachers, the administration,

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:48.900
everyone in that community really pitches

00:29:48.900 --> 00:29:52.100
in to make sure that our
students get what they need.

00:29:52.100 --> 00:29:54.523
And it's been a challenge

00:29:54.523 --> 00:29:56.340
with the pandemic starting

00:29:56.340 --> 00:29:59.480
and teachers really trying to
do everything that they can.

00:29:59.480 --> 00:30:01.030
The teachers at our
school have been amazing

00:30:01.030 --> 00:30:02.630
to really trying to connect with students,

00:30:02.630 --> 00:30:04.830
whether it's through FaceTime
or Twitter, Instagram,

00:30:04.830 --> 00:30:07.681
or any sort of way that that
we can connect with students.

00:30:07.681 --> 00:30:09.730
That's what we've really been trying.

00:30:09.730 --> 00:30:12.380
And it's worked out pretty well for us,

00:30:12.380 --> 00:30:14.020
but it is a huge challenge.

00:30:14.020 --> 00:30:15.630
- Do you think any of your students

00:30:15.630 --> 00:30:19.257
or the other staff members
might be watching right now?

00:30:19.257 --> 00:30:21.980
- Maybe, perhaps, we are
closed from school today,

00:30:21.980 --> 00:30:24.030
so they could possibly be watching.

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:24.863
I'm not sure.

00:30:24.863 --> 00:30:26.820
You know what, I actually know they are,

00:30:26.820 --> 00:30:30.709
because they're right here
in our virtual audience.

00:30:30.709 --> 00:30:33.542
(audience cheers)

00:30:36.570 --> 00:30:41.000
- Oh my God, Harmony, Jaylen, Adrian.

00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:43.938
- Thanks you guys so much for hanging out

00:30:43.938 --> 00:30:47.210
with us and supporting Alejandro.

00:30:47.210 --> 00:30:50.433
So you guys are in virtual learning.

00:30:50.433 --> 00:30:51.266
- Yes, yes.

00:30:51.266 --> 00:30:54.350
What have you guys been
dealing with going virtual?

00:30:54.350 --> 00:30:57.900
- Yeah, I mean, so when the
pandemic first started in March

00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:00.010
it was a huge challenge to make sure

00:31:00.010 --> 00:31:03.010
that students were still receiving
a high quality education.

00:31:03.850 --> 00:31:06.789
And so we really were able to understand

00:31:06.789 --> 00:31:09.670
how many students didn't
have access to internet

00:31:09.670 --> 00:31:12.233
and students didn't
have access to devices,

00:31:13.105 --> 00:31:15.160
and it really was an
eye-opener for a lot of us.

00:31:15.160 --> 00:31:17.070
So, we were rushing and copying packets

00:31:17.070 --> 00:31:18.750
to try to print as much

00:31:18.750 --> 00:31:20.210
work as we can for student teachers,

00:31:20.210 --> 00:31:22.560
we're dropping off
supplies at people's homes.

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:25.320
We were doing all types of
things just to make sure

00:31:25.320 --> 00:31:27.400
that our students still have

00:31:27.400 --> 00:31:28.880
that sense of community that they would

00:31:28.880 --> 00:31:30.490
have when they come to
our school building.

00:31:30.490 --> 00:31:32.509
So it was a huge challenge and especially

00:31:32.509 --> 00:31:36.560
the internet access piece has been huge.

00:31:36.560 --> 00:31:40.200
You know, the digital
divide is such a big part

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:42.441
of the conversations
that we're having now.

00:31:42.441 --> 00:31:44.709
And we're really seeing how it's affected

00:31:44.709 --> 00:31:48.397
students that I teach and
also how it's affected

00:31:48.397 --> 00:31:50.610
students around the country.

00:31:50.610 --> 00:31:54.720
- Well, we actually reached out to ASUS,

00:31:57.234 --> 00:31:59.300
which is a leading tech company,

00:31:59.300 --> 00:32:01.900
driven by innovation and quality products

00:32:01.900 --> 00:32:03.260
for more than 30 years.

00:32:03.260 --> 00:32:05.510
And they're committed
to providing a variety

00:32:05.510 --> 00:32:09.660
of education solutions to
connect students and teachers,

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:12.150
and they heard your incredible story

00:32:12.150 --> 00:32:13.893
and they are giving us enough

00:32:13.893 --> 00:32:17.520
ASUS Chromebooks for every single student

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:20.145
and teacher at Aiton Elementary.

00:32:20.145 --> 00:32:24.740
(audience applauds)
(audience cheers)

00:32:24.740 --> 00:32:25.573
- That's amazing,

00:32:25.573 --> 00:32:28.563
I can't even fathom that.

00:32:28.563 --> 00:32:29.396
This is crazy.

00:32:29.396 --> 00:32:30.229
Meeting Drew Barrymore,

00:32:30.229 --> 00:32:33.200
getting computers for our school.

00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:34.485
- [Drew] Computers for the entire school.

00:32:34.485 --> 00:32:36.410
- I'm at a loss for words.

00:32:36.410 --> 00:32:38.300
- Students and teachers.

00:32:38.300 --> 00:32:39.974
- Oh my goodness.

00:32:39.974 --> 00:32:42.180
- And all that we want to do on this show

00:32:42.180 --> 00:32:45.370
is recognize what's happening around us,

00:32:45.370 --> 00:32:48.800
shine a light on people who are being

00:32:48.800 --> 00:32:52.110
such extraordinary human beings.

00:32:52.110 --> 00:32:54.280
And thank you to ASUS.

00:32:54.280 --> 00:32:56.870
We can aid in the technological aspect

00:32:56.870 --> 00:33:00.433
that you need as a tool to
be the hero that you are.

00:33:01.558 --> 00:33:02.590
- Oh my God, thank you so much.

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:05.840
I can't even, I'm so happy
for everyone at Aiton.

00:33:05.840 --> 00:33:07.560
I mean, this community deserves it.

00:33:07.560 --> 00:33:09.710
They are just such a strong community

00:33:09.710 --> 00:33:11.850
who every single day the students log on

00:33:11.850 --> 00:33:12.870
and they have their uniforms on,

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:13.703
they're in front of the computer.

00:33:13.703 --> 00:33:15.010
No matter the connection,

00:33:15.010 --> 00:33:16.140
no matter if they have a pencil,

00:33:16.140 --> 00:33:16.973
no matter anything.

00:33:16.973 --> 00:33:19.150
And they just are so
passionate about learning.

00:33:19.150 --> 00:33:21.010
So they deserve this so much.

00:33:21.010 --> 00:33:22.276
They truly do.

00:33:22.276 --> 00:33:23.920
(audience applauds)

00:33:23.920 --> 00:33:24.960
- We actually followed up

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:26.560
with Alejandro a few weeks later

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:28.660
to present him with $50,000

00:33:28.660 --> 00:33:31.120
and a full scholarship for him

00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:34.540
to the National University's
Master's Program.

00:33:34.540 --> 00:33:37.010
Gosh, he's so deserves it and more.

00:33:37.010 --> 00:33:37.843
By the way,

00:33:37.843 --> 00:33:39.680
he also won teacher of
the year in his district.

00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:42.056
So I'm just saying, we'll be right back.

00:33:42.056 --> 00:33:44.639
(upbeat music)

00:33:49.068 --> 00:33:51.651
(upbeat music)

00:33:52.910 --> 00:33:54.200
Welcome back.

00:33:54.200 --> 00:33:57.390
Today, we're revisiting our
favorite moments from season

00:33:57.390 --> 00:33:59.430
one of "The Drew Barrymore Show",

00:33:59.430 --> 00:34:02.120
with a little extra
behind the scenes info,

00:34:02.120 --> 00:34:05.330
from yours truly, it's the Barrymore Cut.

00:34:05.330 --> 00:34:07.490
Now, my hands down favorite part

00:34:07.490 --> 00:34:10.440
of the show every day is Drew's News.

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:13.910
And last season I had
one very special guest

00:34:13.910 --> 00:34:17.150
join me at the desk, Gayle King.

00:34:17.150 --> 00:34:21.280
Now this segment aired as like
12 minutes total, I think,

00:34:21.280 --> 00:34:22.790
but we talked for probably,

00:34:22.790 --> 00:34:25.580
I would say for more than almost an hour,

00:34:25.580 --> 00:34:28.070
I could've talked to her for another hour.

00:34:28.070 --> 00:34:29.718
I love Gayle.

00:34:29.718 --> 00:34:31.570
Take a look.

00:34:31.570 --> 00:34:34.510
- I get the first headline
they say, so here we go.

00:34:34.510 --> 00:34:36.370
Starting with the latest vaccine news,

00:34:36.370 --> 00:34:39.700
CBS News reports that
Pfizer has completed a study

00:34:39.700 --> 00:34:41.100
and found their vaccine is safe.

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:43.270
And 100% effective for kids.

00:34:43.270 --> 00:34:45.980
This is big, ages 12 to 15.

00:34:45.980 --> 00:34:48.300
So Pfizer plans to file with the FDA

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:51.585
for emergency use of the
vaccine for kids as young as 12.

00:34:51.585 --> 00:34:53.310
I know, Drew, that there's still

00:34:53.310 --> 00:34:56.915
some vaccine hesitancy,
but that's changing.

00:34:56.915 --> 00:34:58.190
I've heard people say,

00:34:58.190 --> 00:34:59.860
well I never got the flu shot.

00:34:59.860 --> 00:35:02.030
The difference is if you
didn't get the flu shot,

00:35:02.030 --> 00:35:03.620
it only affected you.

00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:05.450
If you don't get this vaccine,

00:35:05.450 --> 00:35:07.640
it affects all of us and
the people around you.

00:35:07.640 --> 00:35:09.800
So I think this is very exciting,

00:35:09.800 --> 00:35:12.440
especially for kids because
they can go back to school.

00:35:12.440 --> 00:35:14.010
I don't know about Frankie and all,

00:35:14.010 --> 00:35:16.610
but aren't they sick of just
looking at a little Zoom, Zoom.

00:35:16.610 --> 00:35:18.340
- Everyone has hit a fatigue level.

00:35:18.340 --> 00:35:20.120
That's so real.

00:35:20.120 --> 00:35:22.200
And we know we're here
for a little longer.

00:35:22.200 --> 00:35:24.210
So the sooner we get vaccinated-

00:35:24.210 --> 00:35:26.470
- The better it'll be to get back to life.

00:35:26.470 --> 00:35:27.580
Have you, I've had mine.

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:28.905
- I've had mine too.
- I've had mine too.

00:35:28.905 --> 00:35:33.500
- In all of this difficult roads

00:35:33.500 --> 00:35:35.020
that we are all navigating.

00:35:35.020 --> 00:35:36.130
I have come to have

00:35:36.130 --> 00:35:38.570
a new re-appreciation for entertainment.

00:35:38.570 --> 00:35:39.403
- Yup, me too.

00:35:39.403 --> 00:35:41.480
- I think when the world falls apart,

00:35:41.480 --> 00:35:43.373
you just think entertainment,

00:35:44.290 --> 00:35:46.200
bigger picture problems.

00:35:46.200 --> 00:35:48.130
And then you're at home for a long time

00:35:48.130 --> 00:35:49.564
and you just need an escape.

00:35:49.564 --> 00:35:50.730
- It's like entertainment, please.

00:35:50.730 --> 00:35:51.720
- Exactly.

00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:53.640
So a show like "Bridgerton",

00:35:53.640 --> 00:35:55.740
the steamy Netflix hit that broke records

00:35:55.740 --> 00:35:57.170
is coming back for season two.

00:35:57.170 --> 00:35:59.080
It's been a personal favorite of mine.

00:35:59.080 --> 00:36:00.840
Glamor reports that the show plans

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:03.330
to take some real life
inspiration from former royals,

00:36:03.330 --> 00:36:05.180
Harry and Megan, and in season two,

00:36:05.180 --> 00:36:08.910
the characters Simon and
Daphne, newlyweds, new parents,

00:36:08.910 --> 00:36:12.440
will go through some struggles
similar to Megan and Harry.

00:36:12.440 --> 00:36:15.870
I think being royals,
having public opinion,

00:36:15.870 --> 00:36:17.720
being new parents are sort

00:36:17.720 --> 00:36:20.340
of the similar through lines.

00:36:20.340 --> 00:36:22.870
Gayle, I can't think of
anyone we've looked to

00:36:22.870 --> 00:36:24.360
for the north star other than you

00:36:24.360 --> 00:36:29.110
and Oprah for all of the
truths from Harry and Megan.

00:36:29.110 --> 00:36:31.790
I think that at interview
opened up conversations

00:36:31.790 --> 00:36:33.680
that many people weren't prepared to have,

00:36:33.680 --> 00:36:35.190
or didn't want to have.

00:36:35.190 --> 00:36:37.550
But I think ultimately it's about a family

00:36:37.550 --> 00:36:39.450
that wants to be united and be together.

00:36:39.450 --> 00:36:41.670
And I'm hoping that that
that will be the case.

00:36:41.670 --> 00:36:44.080
- And have those difficult conversations.

00:36:44.080 --> 00:36:45.586
- Yes, yes.

00:36:45.586 --> 00:36:48.060
And sometimes conversations
are very difficult.

00:36:48.060 --> 00:36:48.893
- Yeah.

00:36:48.893 --> 00:36:51.840
I really admire hearing all the details

00:36:51.840 --> 00:36:54.240
and opening up that important conversation

00:36:54.240 --> 00:36:56.950
and really making history

00:36:56.950 --> 00:36:59.160
in a conversation that was refusing

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:01.380
to be had for centuries.

00:37:01.380 --> 00:37:05.240
So I'm very happy that the lid is off,

00:37:05.240 --> 00:37:07.560
and there's nothing healthy

00:37:07.560 --> 00:37:09.480
about not having those
tough conversations.

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:11.750
- There's nothing healthy
when you don't talk.

00:37:11.750 --> 00:37:14.010
